@@ -251,9 +251,11 @@ The Bash tool's shell, grep, node, a Playwright browser, a worktree dev server:
 
 `node Servex/leaks.mjs` (a leak = a start line with no end line) reads every start and end line from the session logs and the `spawns` log and puts each unended start in a group: **gone, end missed** (the pid is not running: a logging bug), **alive, owner done** (its agent is stopped or dormant, or its task has landed: a real leak) or **alive, owner working** (fine). `--write <file>` writes the markdown report. It never kills anything. Run it, then fix the close path that missed the process (`Lifecycle.js`, `closeout`, `Pool.js`, `Server/Browser.mjs`), never the process itself. Tests: `Servex/leaks.test.mjs` (the pairing), `Servex/chrome-pin.test.mjs` (no ending path ever picks the owner's Chrome, Code or Figma; a Playwright chrome is picked; a forced kill writes its end lines).
 
+**Gone at boot.** A crash (or a hard stop) takes Servex's exit handlers with it, so a process that ended while Servex was down never gets its end line: on 2026-11-08 the four claude.exe started in the 05:05 boot (`assistant-fast`, `master-assistant`, two of `mastermind-session-truth`) died with that boot's 05:06 native crash and stayed open. A minute after every boot, Servex runs `node Servex/leaks.mjs --close-gone --before <boot time>` (`Servex.js`, after `revive()`; `SERVEX_NO_GONE_SWEEP=1` skips it): every gone start older than the boot gets one `{"pid_exit","ended","seen":"boot"}` line in each log its start is in. `ended` is an upper bound (the boot, or 1 ms before the same number started again), not the real exit time. `--dry-run` prints the count without writing. Test: `Servex/leaks.test.mjs` (`close_lines()`).
+
 ### Coverage, and the two gaps that log nothing
 
-`node Servex/pid-coverage.mjs [--hours 24]` sorts every start line in the window by the exe actually spawned (`claude.exe`, `node`, `git`, `powershell/bash`, `chrome (playwright)`, `other`) and prints, per kind, how many got an end line and how many carry both an `agent` and a `session`. Read-only, reuses `pair()`/`read_lines()` from `leaks.mjs`. The table is not committed here — a snapshot drifts the moment it is written, so it lives only in the task report, [pid-accounting/report.md](/framework/ai/sessions/2026/11/06/spawned/mastermind-resources/pid-accounting/report.md); rerun the script for the current numbers. A snapshot taken 2026-11-08: claude.exe ends about 82% (of 6 open starts, 2 were still-live agents and 4 were gone with no end line — a real small logging gap, same shape as "gone, end missed" above) and attributes 100%; chrome ends and attributes 100%; git, node and the rest end near 99% but are attributed only through their nearest tracked ancestor (an agent's own claude.exe), so a line copied to no agent's log (a lone `spawns` poll) shows 0% there by design, not as a bug.
+`node Servex/pid-coverage.mjs [--hours 24]` sorts every start line in the window by the exe actually spawned (`claude.exe`, `node`, `git`, `powershell/bash`, `chrome (playwright)`, `other`) and prints, per kind, how many got an end line and how many carry both an `agent` and a `session`. Read-only, reuses `pair()`/`read_lines()` from `leaks.mjs`. The table is not committed here — a snapshot drifts the moment it is written, so it lives only in the task report, [pid-accounting/report.md](/framework/ai/sessions/2026/11/06/spawned/mastermind-resources/pid-accounting/report.md); rerun the script for the current numbers. A snapshot taken 2026-11-08: claude.exe ends about 82% (of 6 open starts, 2 were still-live agents and 4 were gone with no end line, killed by the 05:06 Servex crash; closed by "Gone at boot" above) and attributes 100%; chrome ends and attributes 100%; git, node and the rest end near 99% but are attributed only through their nearest tracked ancestor (an agent's own claude.exe), so a line copied to no agent's log (a lone `spawns` poll) shows 0% there by design, not as a bug.
 
